---
import ProjectLayout from "../../../layouts/ProjectLayout.astro";
---

<ProjectLayout
	title="Medical Imaging Research"
	category="Machine Learning · Medical AI"
	year="2026"
	summary="Research on longitudinal kidney function, multimodal ultrasound, and the data infrastructure required for reliable medical AI."
>

	<!-- =====================================================
	     01 — OVERARCHING RESEARCH QUESTION
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Research Question
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				The broader problem
			</p>

			<h2>
				Can longitudinal renal function and multimodal ultrasound provide reliable quantitative markers of kidney disease?
			</h2>

			<p class="lead">
				My research combined statistical modeling, medical imaging,
				deep learning, data engineering and quality control to study
				kidney function and tissue characteristics.
			</p>

			<p>
				The work extended beyond training a single model.
				It involved building and validating much of the pipeline
				connecting raw ultrasound exams and longitudinal clinical
				measurements to downstream statistical and machine-learning
				analysis.
			</p>

			<p class="confidentiality-top">
				Patient-level data, study-specific results and confidential
				research information are intentionally omitted.
			</p>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     02 — WHY IT MATTERS
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Why It Matters
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<h2>
				Reliable medical AI starts before the model.
			</h2>

			<p>
				In applied medical imaging research, predictive performance
				is only one part of the problem.
			</p>

			<p>
				Clinical measurements may be irregular, imaging datasets may
				contain missing or mismatched files, segmentation quality may
				vary across cases, and every processing step needs to remain
				traceable.
			</p>

			<p>
				For downstream analysis to be meaningful, acquisition,
				cleaning, preprocessing, modeling and human review all have
				to function as one reproducible research system.
			</p>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     03 — BIG QUESTIONS
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Big Questions
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				The research in four questions
			</p>

			<h2>
				Four questions shaped my work.
			</h2>


			<div class="question-grid">

				<div class="question-card">

					<span class="question-number">
						01
					</span>

					<h3>
						How should longitudinal eGFR trajectories be estimated from irregular clinical measurements?
					</h3>

					<p>
						Compare statistical approaches for estimating
						kidney-function change under different assumptions
						about time, weighting and patient-level variation.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="question-card">

					<span class="question-number">
						02
					</span>

					<h3>
						Can multimodal ultrasound provide predictive information about renal fibrosis?
					</h3>

					<p>
						Evaluate whether B-mode, H-scan and spectral
						representations contain useful signal for fibrosis
						prediction using deep-learning models.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="question-card">

					<span class="question-number">
						03
					</span>

					<h3>
						Can kidney segmentation be automated while preserving effective quality control?
					</h3>

					<p>
						Develop automatic segmentation together with
						quantitative evaluation, visual inspection and
						a practical workflow for reviewing failure cases.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="question-card">

					<span class="question-number">
						04
					</span>

					<h3>
						How can data integrity be maintained from acquisition through analysis?
					</h3>

					<p>
						Automate data acquisition while detecting missing,
						swapped, mismatched or otherwise inconsistent
						imaging data before downstream analysis.
					</p>

				</div>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     04 — RESEARCH MAP
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Research Map
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<h2>
				Five connected workstreams.
			</h2>

			<div class="research-map">

				<div class="map-row">

					<span>
						01
					</span>

					<div>
						<strong>
							eGFR trajectory modeling
						</strong>

						<p>
							Clinical-data cleaning, patient matching and
							longitudinal slope estimation.
						</p>
					</div>

				</div>


				<div class="map-row">

					<span>
						02
					</span>

					<div>
						<strong>
							Fibrosis prediction
						</strong>

						<p>
							Deep learning with multiple ultrasound-derived
							image representations.
						</p>
					</div>

				</div>


				<div class="map-row">

					<span>
						03
					</span>

					<div>
						<strong>
							Automatic kidney segmentation
						</strong>

						<p>
							Model training, inference, quantitative evaluation
							and segmentation quality control.
						</p>
					</div>

				</div>


				<div class="map-row">

					<span>
						04
					</span>

					<div>
						<strong>
							Data integrity
						</strong>

						<p>
							File-level and image-level auditing of legacy
							and redownloaded ultrasound data.
						</p>
					</div>

				</div>


				<div class="map-row">

					<span>
						05
					</span>

					<div>
						<strong>
							Automated data acquisition
						</strong>

						<p>
							Downloading, synchronization, logging and
							packaging of new ultrasound exams.
						</p>
					</div>

				</div>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     05 — eGFR
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			01 — eGFR
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				Longitudinal clinical modeling
			</p>

			<h2>
				Estimating kidney-function trajectories.
			</h2>

			<p>
				I filtered clinical eGFR records to patients with usable
				ultrasound or RF data and matched clinical measurements
				to established patient and imaging timelines.
			</p>

			<p>
				The objective was not simply to calculate one slope.
				I explored multiple approaches for characterizing
				longitudinal kidney-function change and compared the
				assumptions behind different methods.
			</p>


			<div class="method-grid">

				<div class="method-card">

					<span>
						Method
					</span>

					<strong>
						Weighted Least Squares
					</strong>

					<p>
						Regression-based estimation with weighting strategies
						for longitudinal observations.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="method-card">

					<span>
						Method
					</span>

					<strong>
						GAMM
					</strong>

					<p>
						Flexible modeling for trajectories that may not be
						adequately described by a simple linear trend.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="method-card">

					<span>
						Method
					</span>

					<strong>
						LSME / mixed-effects approaches
					</strong>

					<p>
						Methods incorporating repeated observations and
						patient-level variation.
					</p>

				</div>


				<div class="method-card">

					<span>
						Goal
					</span>

					<strong>
						Robust trajectory estimates
					</strong>

					<p>
						Compare alternative representations of renal-function
						change for downstream research analysis.
					</p>

				</div>

			</div>


			<div class="project-tags">

				<span>eGFR</span>
				<span>Longitudinal Data</span>
				<span>WLS</span>
				<span>GAMM</span>
				<span>Mixed Effects</span>
				<span>Statistical Modeling</span>
				<span>Data Cleaning</span>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     06 — FIBROSIS AI
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			02 — Fibrosis AI
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				Multimodal ultrasound learning
			</p>

			<h2>
				Testing ultrasound representations for fibrosis prediction.
			</h2>

			<p>
				I trained deep-learning models to investigate whether
				different ultrasound-derived representations could be used
				to predict kidney fibrosis levels.
			</p>

			<p>
				Rather than treating ultrasound as a single image type,
				the experiments compared information encoded through
				multiple input representations.
			</p>


			<div class="modality-grid">

				<div class="modality-card">

					<span>
						Input 01
					</span>

					<strong>
						B-mode
					</strong>

				</div>


				<div class="modality-card">

					<span>
						Input 02
					</span>

					<strong>
						H-scan
					</strong>

				</div>


				<div class="modality-card">

					<span>
						Input 03
					</span>

					<strong>
						Spectral images
					</strong>

				</div>

			</div>


			<p>
				The modeling work included CNN architectures,
				ResNet-based models, transfer learning, model
				fine-tuning and cross-validation across experimental
				data splits.
			</p>


			<div class="project-tags">

				<span>CNN</span>
				<span>ResNet</span>
				<span>Transfer Learning</span>
				<span>Cross Validation</span>
				<span>B-mode</span>
				<span>H-scan</span>
				<span>Spectral Imaging</span>
				<span>Medical AI</span>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     07 — SEGMENTATION
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			03 — Segmentation
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				Automation + human review
			</p>

			<h2>
				Scaling kidney segmentation without losing quality control.
			</h2>

			<p>
				I updated and extended the kidney-segmentation pipeline
				so ultrasound images could be prepared, processed and
				segmented automatically.
			</p>


			<div class="subsection">

				<h3>
					Automatic segmentation
				</h3>

				<p>
					I worked with machine-learning models for automatic
					kidney segmentation and developed the processing
					workflows required to run segmentation over new
					batches of ultrasound images.
				</p>

			</div>


			<div class="subsection">

				<h3>
					Segmentation evaluation
				</h3>

				<p>
					Predictions were evaluated quantitatively using
					segmentation metrics and visually reviewed to identify
					incorrect or low-quality outputs.
				</p>

			</div>


			<div class="subsection">

				<h3>
					Segmentation review GUI
				</h3>

				<p>
					I built a dedicated graphical interface for reviewing
					segmentation outputs, comparing masks, locating cases,
					recording quality-control decisions and identifying
					images requiring manual correction.
				</p>

			</div>


			<div class="metric-row">

				<span>Dice</span>
				<span>IoU</span>
				<span>Precision</span>
				<span>Recall</span>

			</div>


			<div class="project-tags">

				<span>Segmentation</span>
				<span>Deep Learning</span>
				<span>Image Processing</span>
				<span>GUI Development</span>
				<span>Quality Control</span>
				<span>Human Review</span>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     08 — DATA INTEGRITY
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			04 — Data Integrity
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				Research data engineering
			</p>

			<h2>
				Verifying that the data entering the analysis is the correct data.
			</h2>

			<p>
				I audited the legacy ultrasound dataset against newly
				downloaded RF and ENV data to identify inconsistencies
				that could affect downstream experiments.
			</p>

			<p>
				The comparison workflow operated at both image and file
				level and was designed to surface cases requiring
				targeted remediation.
			</p>


			<div class="integrity-list">

				<div>
					<span>01</span>
					<p>Missing files or image folders</p>
				</div>

				<div>
					<span>02</span>
					<p>Hash and content mismatches</p>
				</div>

				<div>
					<span>03</span>
					<p>Incorrect folder or image associations</p>
				</div>

				<div>
					<span>04</span>
					<p>Image swaps and inconsistent data structure</p>
				</div>

				<div>
					<span>05</span>
					<p>Missing raw-data components</p>
				</div>

			</div>


			<div class="project-tags">

				<span>Data Validation</span>
				<span>Hash Comparison</span>
				<span>RF Data</span>
				<span>ENV Data</span>
				<span>Dataset Auditing</span>
				<span>Python</span>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     09 — AUTOMATED DOWNLOADING
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			05 — Automation
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				Data acquisition infrastructure
			</p>

			<h2>
				Automating the arrival of new ultrasound data.
			</h2>

			<p>
				I built and maintained an automated workflow for
				downloading new ultrasound exams and raw data from
				the Clarius platform.
			</p>

			<p>
				The workflow supported incremental downloading and
				included persistent synchronization state, logging and
				fixes for exam-date inconsistencies.
			</p>

			<p>
				I later packaged the workflow as a standalone desktop
				application, turning an internal research script into
				a reusable tool that could be operated without requiring
				users to manually run a Python environment.
			</p>


			<!-- PUBLIC SOFTWARE RELEASE -->

			<div class="release-card">

				<div class="release-top">

					<div>

						<p class="release-label">
							Public Tool
						</p>

						<h3>
							ClariusRawDownloader
						</h3>

						<p class="release-description">
							A standalone desktop application for downloading
							Clarius Cloud RAW ultrasound data. The application
							packages the automated acquisition workflow into
							an interface that can be used without manually
							running the underlying Python scripts.
						</p>

					</div>


					<div class="release-version">
						<span class="release-status"></span>
						Latest release
					</div>

				</div>


				<div class="release-actions">

					<a
						class="release-button release-primary"
						href="https://github.com/Shercroft/ClariusRawDownloader/releases/latest/download/ClariusRawDownloader-Setup.exe"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer"
					>
						Download for Windows
						<span>↓</span>
					</a>

					<a
						class="release-button"
						href="https://github.com/Shercroft/ClariusRawDownloader/releases/latest"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer"
					>
						All releases
						<span>↗</span>
					</a>

					<a
						class="release-button"
						href="https://github.com/Shercroft/ClariusRawDownloader"
						target="_blank"
						rel="noopener noreferrer"
					>
						GitHub
						<span>↗</span>
					</a>

				</div>

			</div>


			<div class="project-tags">

				<span>Python</span>
				<span>Automation</span>
				<span>Data Acquisition</span>
				<span>Logging</span>
				<span>Synchronization</span>
				<span>State Management</span>
				<span>Desktop Application</span>
				<span>Windows Packaging</span>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     10 — END-TO-END PIPELINE
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			The Pipeline
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<p class="eyebrow">
				End-to-end workflow
			</p>

			<h2>
				From acquisition to analysis.
			</h2>


			<div class="pipeline">

				<div class="pipeline-step">

					<span class="pipeline-number">
						01
					</span>

					<div class="pipeline-content">

						<h3>
							Download
						</h3>

						<p>
							Automatically acquire new ultrasound exams
							and raw data.
						</p>

					</div>

				</div>


				<div class="pipeline-step">

					<span class="pipeline-number">
						02
					</span>

					<div class="pipeline-content">

						<h3>
							Audit
						</h3>

						<p>
							Detect missing, swapped or mismatched
							imaging data.
						</p>

					</div>

				</div>


				<div class="pipeline-step">

					<span class="pipeline-number">
						03
					</span>

					<div class="pipeline-content">

						<h3>
							Prepare
						</h3>

						<p>
							Transform raw imaging data into
							analysis-ready inputs.
						</p>

					</div>

				</div>


				<div class="pipeline-step">

					<span class="pipeline-number">
						04
					</span>

					<div class="pipeline-content">

						<h3>
							Segment
						</h3>

						<p>
							Automatically identify kidney regions
							in ultrasound images.
						</p>

					</div>

				</div>


				<div class="pipeline-step">

					<span class="pipeline-number">
						05
					</span>

					<div class="pipeline-content">

						<h3>
							Review
						</h3>

						<p>
							Evaluate segmentation quality and identify
							cases requiring correction.
						</p>

					</div>

				</div>


				<div class="pipeline-step">

					<span class="pipeline-number">
						06
					</span>

					<div class="pipeline-content">

						<h3>
							Analyze
						</h3>

						<p>
							Model longitudinal eGFR trajectories and
							investigate fibrosis prediction.
						</p>

					</div>

				</div>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     11 — TOOLS
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Tools & Methods
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<h2>
				Across statistics, machine learning and data engineering.
			</h2>


			<div class="project-tags tools">

				<span>Python</span>
				<span>Pandas</span>
				<span>NumPy</span>
				<span>TensorFlow / Keras</span>
				<span>ResNet</span>
				<span>CNN</span>
				<span>Transfer Learning</span>
				<span>Cross Validation</span>
				<span>Longitudinal Modeling</span>
				<span>WLS</span>
				<span>GAMM</span>
				<span>Mixed Effects</span>
				<span>Image Processing</span>
				<span>Segmentation</span>
				<span>Git</span>

			</div>

		</div>

	</div>


	<!-- =====================================================
	     12 — TAKEAWAY
	===================================================== -->

	<div class="project-block">

		<div class="project-label">
			Takeaway
		</div>

		<div class="project-main">

			<h2>
				The model is only one part of the research system.
			</h2>

			<p class="lead">
				This work reinforced that reliable medical AI depends
				on the entire chain surrounding the model.
			</p>

			<p>
				Reliable predictions require trustworthy acquisition,
				correct data, reproducible preprocessing, appropriate
				statistical methods, robust evaluation and careful
				human review.
			</p>

			<p>
				My role therefore evolved from individual analysis tasks
				into developing and validating much of the infrastructure
				connecting raw ultrasound data to downstream research
				questions.
			</p>


			<p class="confidentiality">
				Patient-level data, study-specific results and other
				confidential research information are intentionally omitted.
			</p>

		</div>

	</div>


